    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The Asphalt Jungle (1950) revolutionized the heist film genre with its gritty realism and complex character studies. Director John Huston stripped away the glamour of crime, showing instead the desperate mechanics of a jewel robbery and its devastating aftermath. The film influenced countless successors, from <a href=\"/movie/934\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"934\">Rififi</a> (1955) to <a href=\"/movie/299\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"299\">Ocean's Eleven</a> (1960), establishing core heist movie elements: the crew assembly, the detailed planning, the tense execution, and the inevitable unraveling. Sterling Hayden's performance as Dix Handley set the template for the tough-but-doomed criminal archetype."
      },
      {
