      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Huston's innovative decision to focus on the criminals' personal lives rather than just the crime itself created a new paradigm for crime films. Each character in The Asphalt Jungle represents a different facet of human weakness: greed, lust, pride, or desperation. This psychological approach influenced later classics like <a href=\"/movie/968\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"968\">Dog Day Afternoon</a> (1975) and <a href=\"/movie/949\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"949\">Heat</a> (1995), where the criminals' personal dramas become as important as their crimes. The film also launched Marilyn Monroe's career with her small but memorable role as Angela Phinlay."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's visual style, crafted by cinematographer Harold Rosson, established a new template for urban crime cinema. The stark shadows and location shooting in Cincinnati created a documentary-like atmosphere that influenced the French New Wave, particularly films like <a href=\"/movie/26030\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"26030\">Bob le Flambeur</a> (1956). This realistic approach can be traced through <a href=\"/movie/25680\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"25680\">The Friends of Eddie Coyle</a> (1973) to modern crime dramas like <a href=\"/movie/64690\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"64690\">Drive</a> (2011). SUBHEAD: Impact on Modern Crime Cinema"
      },
